The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),

Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force and the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Gravely concerned by the continuing escalation of violence in the Middle East and the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including heavy civilian casualties, the destruction of civilian infrastructure, the collapse of health services, the shortage of food, water, fuel, medicine and electricity, and the displacement of civilians,

Condemning all attacks against civilians and civilian objects, all indiscriminate attacks, all acts of terrorism, and all acts of hostage-taking, and demanding the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages,

Emphasizing that hospitals, medical personnel, humanitarian workers, United Nations personnel, journalists and civilian objects must be protected, and that humanitarian access must be safe, rapid and unhindered,

Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, and stressing that any measures taken in self-defence must comply with international humanitarian law,

Stressing the need to avoid forcible displacement of the Palestinian civilian population and to ensure that any movement of civilians is voluntary and safe,

Reaffirming its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, based on the 4 June 1967 lines and relevant agreements,

Welcoming diplomatic efforts by Egypt, Qatar, the United Nations and other regional and international partners to ease the humanitarian situation and secure the release of hostages,

1. Calls for urgent and extended humanitarian pauses and humanitarian corridors throughout the Gaza Strip for a sufficient number of days to enable full, rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access and the delivery of essential supplies, and calls upon all parties to create the conditions for a sustainable cessation of hostilities;
2. Condemns all violence against civilians, all indiscriminate attacks and all acts of terrorism, and demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precaution;
3. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages, and calls for humane treatment of and access to hostages by medical and humanitarian personnel;
4. Demands safe, rapid, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip, including fuel, food, water, medical supplies and electricity, and calls for the opening and sustained use of humanitarian corridors, including the Rafah crossing, under United Nations coordination;
5. Demands that all parties protect civilians, medical personnel, hospitals, humanitarian workers, United Nations personnel, journalists and civilian infrastructure, and refrain from attacks against civilian objects, including schools and medical facilities;
6. Calls upon all parties to refrain from acts of forced displacement of the Palestinian civilian population and to ensure the safety and voluntary nature of any movement of civilians;
7. Calls for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and all attacks against civilians, and stresses the need for independent and impartial investigations;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 72 hours and every 30 days thereafter on the implementation of this resolution and on the humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip;
9. Reaffirms its commitment to a comprehensive political process aimed at achieving a just, lasting and comprehensive peace in the Middle East based on relevant United Nations resolutions, the Madrid principles, including the principle of land for peace, and the two-State solution, and calls for renewed diplomatic efforts to that end;
10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),

Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force and the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,

Gravely concerned by the continuing escalation of violence in the Middle East and the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including heavy civilian casualties, the destruction of civilian infrastructure, the collapse of health services, the shortage of food, water, fuel, medicine and electricity, and the displacement of civilians,

Condemning in the strongest terms the terrorist attacks by Hamas against Israel on 7 October 2023, and condemning all attacks against civilians and civilian objects, all indiscriminate attacks, all acts of terrorism, including indiscriminate rocket fire against Israel, and all acts of hostage-taking, and demanding the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages,

Emphasizing that hospitals, medical personnel, humanitarian workers, United Nations personnel, journalists and civilian objects must be protected, and that humanitarian access must be safe, rapid and unhindered,

Reaffirming the inherent right of all States, including Israel, to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations, and stressing that any measures taken in self-defence must comply with international humanitarian law,

Stressing the need to avoid forcible displacement of the Palestinian civilian population and to ensure that any movement of civilians is voluntary and safe,

Stressing the urgent need for de-escalation and a cessation of hostilities to alleviate the humanitarian situation,

Reaffirming its vision of a region where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, based on the 4 June 1967 lines and relevant agreements,

Welcoming diplomatic efforts by Egypt, Qatar, the United Nations and other regional and international partners to ease the humanitarian situation and secure the release of hostages,

1. Calls for immediate and extended humanitarian pauses and humanitarian corridors throughout the Gaza Strip for a sufficient number of days to enable full, rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access and the delivery of essential supplies, and calls upon all parties to ensure that these humanitarian pauses are respected and can be extended as needed; underscores that these measures are humanitarian in nature and do not constitute a ceasefire and do not limit the right of all States, including Israel, to self-defence in accordance with the Charter;

2. Condemns unequivocally the heinous terrorist attacks by Hamas and other terrorist groups against Israel that began on 7 October 2023, and the taking of hostages; condemns all violence against civilians, all indiscriminate attacks and all acts of terrorism; and demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precaution;

3. Demands the immediate, safe and unconditional release of all hostages, without preconditions, and calls for the International Committee of the Red Cross to be granted immediate access to all hostages;

4. Demands safe, rapid, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip, including fuel, food, water, medical supplies and electricity, including to hospitals, UNRWA facilities and civilian shelters, and calls for the opening and sustained use of all crossings and humanitarian corridors, including the Rafah crossing and other crossing points, under United Nations coordination, without conditions, and with respect for humanitarian notification and deconfliction arrangements; calls for the removal of restrictions on the movement of humanitarian goods and personnel and for the sustained entry of fuel sufficient to restore hospitals, water and electricity; calls for safe medical evacuations; and requests the Secretary-General to establish appropriate monitoring arrangements, in coordination with relevant parties, to ensure humanitarian supplies are delivered to civilians and not diverted;

5. Demands that all parties protect civilians, medical personnel, hospitals, humanitarian workers, United Nations personnel, journalists and civilian infrastructure, and refrain from attacks against civilian objects, including schools and medical facilities;

6. Calls upon all parties to refrain from acts of forced displacement of the Palestinian civilian population within Gaza or outside Gaza; rejects any forced displacement, transfer or relocation; calls for the rescission of the order for civilians in northern Gaza to move to the south, ensuring humanitarian access to those who remain and safe return for displaced civilians; stresses that any movement of civilians must be voluntary, safe and in accordance with international humanitarian law; and calls for displaced civilians to return voluntarily, safely and with dignity to their homes as soon as conditions permit;

7. Condemns Hamas's use of civilians and civilian infrastructure as human shields, and calls upon all parties to take all feasible precautions to protect civilians, including by not locating military objectives in densely populated areas;

8. Calls for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and all attacks against civilians, including through independent and impartial investigations, including the attack on the Al-Ahli Arab Hospital, and stresses the need for independent and impartial investigations;

9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 72 hours and every 30 days thereafter on the implementation of this resolution and on the humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip;

10. Reaffirms its commitment to a comprehensive political process aimed at achieving a just, lasting and comprehensive peace in the Middle East based on relevant United Nations resolutions, the Madrid principles, including the principle of land for peace, and the two-State solution, and calls for renewed diplomatic efforts to end the Israeli occupation that began in 1967 and to realize the independent State of Palestine on the basis of the 1967 lines with East Jerusalem as its capital;

11. Calls upon all parties to refrain from incitement and provocative actions and to engage in good-faith diplomatic efforts, including through the United Nations, Egypt, Qatar and other regional partners, to de-escalate and revive a credible peace process;

12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

